outputs.L1:
**A flexible pipe connector (200) for joining a pressurised mains pipe to a service pipe, the connector comprising:**

- **a corrugated longitudinal body portion (202)** having a first end (208), a second end (210), and an internal bore extending therebetween, said longitudinal body portion further comprising a plurality of corrugations spaced along its length, wherein the corrugations define a maximum internal diameter of the bore at a peak (204) of a corrugation and a minimum internal diameter of the bore at a trough (206) of a corrugation;

- **a first spigot socket (212)** disposed at said first end of the longitudinal body portion; and

- **a second spigot socket (214)** disposed at said second end of the longitudinal body portion;

- **wherein,** for a defined volumetric flow rate, the head loss of a fluid flowing through said body portion is no greater than that which would be incurred were the fluid flowing through a smooth-bored pipe of equal length and having an internal diameter equal to that of the first and second spigot sockets; and

- **wherein** the minimum internal diameter of said corrugated internal bore is greater than the maximum internal diameter of said first and second spigots.

outputs.L2:
A pliable pipe coupling (200) designed to connect a pressurised main pipe with a service line. This coupling consists of:

• An elongated ribbed body section (202) featuring a first extremity (208), a second extremity (210), and a hollow channel running between them. This elongated section also incorporates numerous ridges distributed throughout its length. These ridges establish the widest inner diameter of the channel where a ridge reaches its peak (204), and the narrowest inner diameter of the channel where a ridge dips to its trough (206);

• A first spigot receptacle (212) located at the first extremity of the elongated body section; and

• A second spigot receptacle (214) positioned at the second extremity of the elongated body section;

• In this design, for any given rate of volume flow, the pressure loss experienced by fluid passing through the body section will not exceed what would occur if that same fluid travelled through a smooth-walled pipe of identical length whose inner diameter matched that of the first and second spigot receptacles; and

• Furthermore, the narrowest inner diameter of the ribbed channel exceeds the widest outer diameter of both the first and second spigots.
